## Heuristic → reported effect

| Heuristic | Reported effect |
|---|---|
| Stop-gradient | Required: SimSiam without it 0.1; BYOL τ = 0 → 0.3 |
| EMA | τ = 1 → 18.8; τ ∈ 0.9–0.999 good (BYOL). DINO without momentum → 0.1. data2vec: τ too low → collapse. DirectPred on CIFAR without EMA: 92 → 12 (Halvagal) |
| Predictor presence | Required for BYOL / SimSiam; a fixed random predictor fails; not needed with SIGReg |
| Predictor lr | Without EMA, needs ≥2× the base lr (BYOL); collapse if predictor lr ≤ projector lr |
| Predictor depth | Deeper is better in I-JEPA (+2.9), A-JEPA and Point-JEPA; Graph-JEPA argues the opposite |
| Predictor width | A bottleneck helps (I-JEPA 384 > 1024; SimSiam d/4) |
| Masking | I-JEPA target scale peaks at (0.15, 0.2); needs ≥3–4 blocks. V-JEPA best near 90%. Multi-block ≫ random. I-JEPA rank is sensitive to mask scale (LiDAR) |
| Weight decay | I-JEPA ramp 0.04 → 0.4 vs fixed is mixed. BYOL 0 is unstable. DirectPred: large η → collapse |
| Rank over training (I-JEPA) | Rises, then falls; best accuracy is not at max rank |

## Recommended evaluation conventions

1. Log RankMe (ε = 1e-7, ~25k held-out samples, pooled encoder output) and LiDAR over training, for context and target encoders and the predictor output.
2. Plot spectra in the Jing et al. style at several checkpoints and for each setting.
3. Report the SimSiam std against 1/√d, and the training loss.
4. Use a frozen linear probe as the main metric and k-NN as a monitor. Report mean ± std over ≥3 seeds.
5. Benchmarks: CIFAR-10/100 or STL-10 with ResNet-18 or ViT-T/S, or ImageNet-100 / ImageNette. Absolute I-JEPA numbers are weak at small scale.
6. Mirror the practice sweeps:
   - EMA τ ∈ {0, 0.9, 0.99, 0.996, 0.999, 1}
   - predictor depth, width and lr multiplier
   - target scale / number of blocks / mask ratio
   - weight decay
   - shared weights

   Report collapse as phase diagrams.
